From 250a4978ecc4d7cb169062de70e5684976775b6a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "keepup[bot]" <keepup[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] feat: add EmbeddingGemma 2 to the native embedder

The ONNX build, whose Hugging Face repo was created on 4 Oct 2026, could not
run on 4.3.0, which asks for image, video and audio inputs.
@huggingface/transformers 4.3.1, released on 6 Oct 2026, is the first version
that supports it, so bump to it and add the model to the picker.
all-MiniLM-L6-v2 stays the default.

The model is multimodal and the feature-extraction pipeline loads every
encoder in its config. Remove the vision and audio configs so only the 4-bit
text model is downloaded and loaded, and use the query and document prefixes
from the model card.

The tokenizer config sets model_max_length to 1e+30, so the pipeline's
truncation does nothing for this model. The text splitter holds document
chunks to 2,000 characters, but a chat message goes straight to
embedTextInput, and one long message ran a 3.5 GB container out of memory. So
embedTextInput cuts this model's queries to the same 2,000 characters. The
chat itself keeps the whole message, and the other models' tokenizers already
cut theirs, so they don't change.

The settings page already warns before changing the embedder model: it
clears all embeddings and removes every document from every workspace, in
both directions, and nothing re-embeds until documents are added again. So
this adds no new warning, and the model's description says the same.

This is the first model that isn't on the fallback host, which answers 404
for it, so three things that were only safe while every model was mirrored
change. With Hugging Face blocked, picking it through updateENV ended the
server after the documents were cleared, because the background download's
rejection went unhandled: catch and log it. On 4.3.1 that catch can't see a
failed download of the .onnx_data weights: the library's own rejection goes
unhandled, and in our test it still ended the process. env is shared by every
load, so one fallback sent every later load in the process to the fallback
host, and the model stayed unloadable until a restart: each attempt now starts
from the primary host. And this model skips the fallback, so the error people
see is Hugging Face's, not a 404 from a host that never had it.

